\par\par \b \uc2  Indian official urges  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  to defend rupee. \b0\par\par\uc2 216 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI, Feb 6 (Reuter) - A senior Indian government official, concerned over the fall of the rupee to record lows against the U.S. dollar, accused the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday of failing to defend the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Commerce Secretary Tejendra Khanna, the ministry's highest ranking civil servant, said it was high time the Reserve Bank of India put its foot down against speculators banking on the rupee's decline by selling dollars. \par \par \uc2  Khanna spoke to Reuters in a telephone interview after the rupee had fallen to a record low of 38.35 per dollar, as the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On Monday the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost 2.6 percent of its value, closing at about 37.80. \par \par \uc2  "The ideal parking slot of the rupee is between 34.50 and 35.50 (per U.S. dollar)," Khanna said, accusing the RBI of failing to do its job of defending the stability of the rupee. \par \par \uc2  "It's high time they (RBI) put their foot down and offloaded dollars to put the rupee back into its proper exchange rate," he said. \par \par \uc2  After Khanna made his comments, the rupee quickly recovered to 37.55 per dollar before easing to 37.90. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds26039p5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
