\par\par \b \uc2  Official says rupee should be at 34.50-35.50/dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2 218 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI, Feb 6 (Reuter) - Indian Commerce Secretary Tejendra Khanna said on Tuesday that the appropriate value of the rupee was between 34.50 and 35.50 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Khanna, the top bureaucrat in the ministry, told Reuters in a telephone interview that it was "high time" the Reserve Bank of India put its foot down against \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators by selling dollars. \par \par \uc2  Khanna spoke after the rupee fell to a fresh low of 38.50 to the dollar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refraining from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Commerce Secretary said the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) had failed to meet its goal of defending a stable rupee. \par \par \uc2  "The ideal parking slot of the rupee is between 34.50 and 35.50 (per U.S. dollar)," Khanna said. \par \par \uc2  "It's high time they (RBI) put their foot down and offloaded dollars to put the rupee back into its proper exchange rate," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Indian official said speculators had pushed the rupee down in recent days. \par \par \uc2  "The RBI has said it would defend the stability of the rupee," he said. "Obviously they have failed to meet their objective. They need to get on with the job and do it more effectively." \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds26039p3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
