\par\par \b \uc2  Indian Rupee Plunges As RBI Withdraws From Market \b0\par\par\uc2 211 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 1996\par 11:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI (AP-Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee plunged Monday after the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) stopped its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, dropping nearly 3% on the day and leaving traders and analysts uncertain about the next move in the ongoing rupee crisis. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that the dollar opened relatively stable at around 36.80 rupees, but then rose toward a key psychological level at 37.00 rupees on strong demand from a big state bank. \par \par \uc2  Initially, a round of dollar sales from the Reserve Bank helped to keep the dollar below 37.00 rupees, traders said. But as the demand persisted, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  withdrew from the market, allowing the dollar to skyrocket to an all-time high of 38.10 rupees before it came back slightly. \par \par \uc2  A spokeswoman for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed that it hadn't acted in the market after the round of dollar sales in the morning. She declined to provide any explanation of the reasoning behind the move, however. \par \par \uc2  At the 1030 GMT close of interbank trading, the dollar was trading at 37.85 rupees, up nearly 3% from 36.78 rupees late Friday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 05-02-96 \par \par \uc2  1057GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds25010e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
