\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee fall seen narrowing trade deficit. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shyam Kumar \par \uc2 542 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Jan 31 (Reuter) - The Indian rupee's rapid slide against the dollar need not ring alarm bells as industry stands largely to benefit and a slowdown in imports could narrow the trade deficit, analysts said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "It may narrow the deficit between imports and exports," said R. Balakrishnan, head of research at DSP Financial Consultants, the Indian associate of Merrill Lynch. \par \par \uc2  The trade gap for the year ending on March 31, 1996, is officially projected at $4.0 billion but many analysts had expected it to exceed this figure. \par \par \uc2  The rupee started falling in August 1995 after holding steady at 31.37 per dollar for almost two years. It fell to an all-time low of 36.63/70 on Wednesday despite sporadic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I think this level is comfortable," said Balakrishnan. "Further fall will mean the rupee is undervalued." \par \par \uc2  Ashwini Agarwal, head of research at Indian investment firm SSKI Securities, said the fall looked bad. "But if you examine it, is is healthy for domestic industry." \par \par \uc2  Agarwal said the depreciation meant added protection for Indian manufacturers from imports. Balakrishnan said Indian firms producing commodity plastics and man-made fibres, like Reliance Industries, and Indian Petrochemicals Corporation, would benefit from the rupee's fall. \par \par \uc2  Agarwal said the fear of a slowdown in foreign investment was unwarranted as overseas investors had built in depreciation of the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their calculations. \par \par \uc2  "In the long term, it will be beneficial," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the fall of the rupee was negative for the government as it would see external debt payments rise in rupee terms, driving the fiscal deficit higher. \par \par \uc2  Another negative for the economy is the fear that the weakening rupee will fuel inflation, which has been contained to single-digit levels for the last several months. \par \par \uc2  Annual inflation as measured by the wholesale price index stands at 5.08 percent against 11.89 percent a year ago. \par \par \uc2  India has not revised its government-fixed petroleum product prices for over a year. Petroleum products account for around one-fifth of the country's total import bill. \par \par \uc2  Many analysts believe the government has postponed revision of petroleum prices because of general elections expected in April and due by mid-1996. \par \par \uc2  Bank dealers said they would not rule out a further fall in the value of the rupee because of a near-panic. \par \par \uc2  "Potentially it could shoot out to 38 per dollar in the middle of the year if there is no majority government after April's parliament election, but by the year-end you can see it recovering," Chris Tinker, treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank, told Reuters in London on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Leading Indian firms are covering their import payables in the forward market as they fear the rupee could fall further. \par \par \uc2  Exporters, expecting the rupee to fall further, are still not converting their dollars into rupees in the spot market. \par \par \uc2  "We were selling some dollars for August-delivery at 39.20 in the morning," said an executive of an Indian firm. "We have now raised it to 39.50," he said. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay newsroom +91-22-285 2851 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds1v01bxx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
