\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee under pressure as importers panic. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dev Varam \par \uc2 442 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Jan 8 (Reuter) - Panic buying of dollars by importers on Monday kept the Indian rupee under steady pressure despite an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) to arrest its fall, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Importers are panicking and buying dollars," said a dealer with a foreign bank. "It appears there is a general lack of confidence in the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the RBI had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market as the rupee slipped to 35.54/57 per dollar in early trade from the opening 35.45/47. It fell further to 35.68 per dollar at midsession rendering RBI's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost ineffective. \par \par \uc2  "The rupee is under pressure and will continue to be so unless something drastic is done," said another dealer. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the RBI had been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a daily basis and on Monday corporate demand for dollars appeared to have exceeded the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  A spokesperson for the RBI confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but declined to give any figure. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there had been speculation that some state-run firms, including the Oil and Natural Gas Corporation and the Indian Oil Coporation, were in the market for dollars. \par \par \uc2  Market analysts estimated the RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be in the region of $40-50 million though some dealers put the figure as high as $90-100 million. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll in late December quoted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts as saying that the rupee would slide by at least 5.6 percent against the dollar in 1996. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, bankers said the new overnight limits for banks set by the RBI would add depth to India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market amid renewed downward pressure on the rupee. \par \par \uc2  The RBI has scrapped a system limiting all banks to an outstanding overnight \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  position equivalent to 150 million rupees and instead is authorising different levels for different banks based on their capital. \par \par \uc2  "Some major banks will get a large hike in the outstanding positions they are allowed," said one treasury manager. \par \par \uc2  Some bankers see the relaxation by the RBI adding to the volatility of the rupee, although others say the greater depth to the market could help iron out end-of-day fluctuations. \par \par \uc2  Demand for dollars in India is still mostly determined by export and import demand, with the rupee often fluctuating in line with major Indian oil purchases. Bankers say the market has a daily turnover of between $400 million and $1.0 billion. \par \par \uc2  While there is scope for speculation, bankers say the trend of the rupee is still driven by corporate demand. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds1800m4c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
