\par\par \b \uc2  Indian Rupee Falls Again Despite RBI  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 218 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 1995\par 11:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI (AP-DJ)--The Indian rupee continued its recent slide against the dollar Friday, despite further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) in support of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Money market rates also jumped in early trading Friday and remained fairly strong throughout the day, despite a move by the RBI to inject funds through its affiliated institutions. \par \par \uc2  Traders said strong demand for dollars early in the day lifted the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the rupee Friday, despite RBI sales of dollars around 35.15 rupees. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the demand was spread among a number of banks, and indicated that many players were seeking dollars to pay end-of-year import bills. \par \par \uc2  'Everyone was buying and no one was selling,' a trader with a European bank in Bombay said, adding that the demand overwhelmed the dollar sales by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The buying pushed the dollar to around 35.19 rupees by late Friday, up from 35.13 rupees late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders were evenly divided about the outlook for next week, however. Some dealers predicted that Friday's dollar buying could spill over into next week's dealings, while others noted that dollar buying traditionally dries up in early January. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 29-12-95 \par \par \uc2  1045GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drct0a89i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\pi