\par\par \b \uc2  Indian Rupee Opens Slightly Weaker; Watching For RBI \b0\par\par\uc2 212 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 1995\par 06:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI (AP-DJ)--The Indian rupee opened slightly weaker against the dollar in slow early trading Friday, as players continued to watch for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said demand for dollars continues to lift the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with most of the buying attributed to oil companies, which buy large amounts of dollars each month to pay import bills. \par \par \uc2  But traders said they expect the RBI to continue to sell dollars into the market whenever necessary to make sure that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't rise above 35.00 rupees, a level that the RBI seems to have set as a ceiling for the dollar for the time being. RBI deputy governor S.S. Tarapore told AP-Dow Jones last week that there is no reason for the dollar to rise above 35.00 rupees for the time being. \par \par \uc2  The RBI confirmed that it sold dollars for rupees Thursday, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't been seen in the market yet Friday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  At 0500 GMT, the dollar was trading at 34.9650 rupees, slightly stronger than 34.95 rupees late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 15-12-95 \par \par \uc2  0510GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drcf0a404\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
