\par\par \b \uc2  STATEMENT BY INDIAN FINANCE MINISTER MANMOHAN SINGH. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,168 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  Following is the text of a statement by Indian Finance Minister Manmohan Singh in the Rajya Sabha, the upper house of the Indian parliament, on the recent fall in the value of the rupee against the dollar. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab      -----oOo-----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  In recent weeks there has been concern over the movement in the exchange rate of the rupee. After a prolonged period of stability between March 1993 and July 1995, the exchange rate of the rupee against the U.S. dollar depreciated by 10 percent between end-July 1995 and November 29, 1995. I would like to put these matters in a proper perspective. \par \par \uc2  All over the world, countries have shifted away from the older system of maintaining fixed exchange rates to more flexible systems in which the exchange rate is not rigidly fixed but moves from day reflecting market conditions. \par \par \uc2  India moved to this system in March 1993, when the earlier dual exchange rate regime was unified into a single, market-determined exchange rate system. Under this system there is no officially fixed exchange rate of the rupee. \par \par \uc2  Instead, the rate is determined by demand and supply conditions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market while the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain orderly market conditions and to curb excessive speculation. \par \par \uc2  Despite the shift to a market-determined system, the rupee-dollar rate remained steady at about 31.5 rupees for over two years after March 1993 until July 1995. This happened mainly because there was a large inflow of foreign portfolio investment in 1993 and most of 1994. \par \par \uc2  If the rupee had been left to be determined solely by the market supply and demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this period, it would have appreciated sharply against the dollar. This would have hurt exports and encouraged imports. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2  HIGHER \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  RESERVES \par \par \uc2  It was therefore decided to prevent appreciation and the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by buying dollars in the market. Between March 1993 and December 1994, $13 billion were added to the country's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves because of this process. \par \par \uc2  Towards the end of 1994, the inflow of portfolio investment slowed down considerably, and the upward pressure on the rupee from this source was eliminated. \par \par \uc2  The economy also picked up strongly in 1994/95, with industry growing at eight percent, a strong revival of investment and an overall GDP growth of 6.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  It was expected that the revival of the economy would generate a larger demand for imports and the balance of payments deficit on the current account would widen. This is exactly what happened. The current account deficit increased from abnormally low level of 0.1 percent of GDP in 1993/94 to 0.7 percent in 1994/95. \par \par \uc2  It is expected to increase further to about 1.5 percent of GDP in 1995/96. \par \par \uc2  This is a normal level of current account deficit in our circumstances and is sustainable by medium and long term capital inflows. \par \par \uc2  The reduction in capital inflows and the widening of the current account deficit ended the earlier period of surplus dollar availability. Against this background, the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of normal supply and demand conditions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets gave rise to expectations of a depreciation of the exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  The exchange rate behaviour in recent months was a response to these expectations, though the extent of fluctuations was accentuated by speculative factors. \par \par \uc2  I would like to emphasise that despite the uncertainty created by fluctuations in the exchange rate, the overall direction of the movement of the exchange rate is consistent with the longer term objective of a viable balance of payments based on strong competitiveness of our exports. \par \par \uc2  One of the fundamental factors which undoubtedly influenced the movement in the exchange rate after the middle of 1995 was the fact that during the prolonged period of stability of the rupee-dollar rate from March 1993 to July 1995, our competitiveness in international markets was significantly eroded by the rate of inflation being higher than the rates of inflation of our major trading partners. \par \par \uc2  As a result, the index of the real effective exchange rate, which is obtained by adjusting the index of nominal effective exchange rate (using a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) by the differential rates of inflation (in India as compared to these major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) showed an appreciation of India's exchange rate in real terms in the order of seven percent between 1993-94 and August 1995. \par \par \uc2  This led to some erosion India's competitiveness. Had it continued, it would have adversely affected exports, the foreign trade balance and the overall balance of payments. \par \par \uc2  The market-triggered adjustment in the exchange rate which occurred after August 1995 led to an exchange rate which at present levels brings the real effective exchange rate back to approximately the same level as for 1993-94. \par \par \uc2  In other words, the recent change in the exchange rate has corrected the erosion in competitiveness and will help ensure that export growth remains strong and import growth is contained. Both factors will help to contain the trade deficit to manageable levels. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2  RBI ROLE \par \par \uc2  I would like to say a few words on the role of the Reserve Bank during the recent exchange rate volatility. As I have mentioned the role of the central \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  in modern exchange markets is to ensure orderly market conditions and to counter undue speculation. \par \par \uc2  From April 1993 to October 1994 the Reserve Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent undue appreciation of the rupee. Thereafter purchases by the Reserve Bank was small. \par \par \uc2  Initially, the normal forces of demand and supply ensured that the rupee-dollar rate remained stable despite the tapering off of purchases by the Reserve Bank. \par \par \uc2  However, these very factors subsequently led to pressure on the exchange rate, leading to depreciation of the rupee. Initially , the downward movemnt in the rupee did not warrant any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the RBI in view of the need to correct the real appreciation that had taken place since 1993/94. \par \par \uc2  However, as often happens when the rate changes after a long period of staticity, the change in the exchange rate triggered some speculative factors which, if unchecked, would have caused excessive depreciation. \par \par \uc2  Therefore, the Reserve Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisively when the exchange rate of the rupee reached 35.80 rupees per US dollar in late October, 1995. As a result the rupee bounced back and has remained stable since then between 34.50 and 35 rupees per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to monitor exchange market developments and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as necessary to ensure that market fluctuations are contained within reasonable levels and are consistent with economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  However, we must recognise that the world has moved away from fixed exchange rates and we should expect the exchange rate to vary in response to economic fundamentals. The real economy of the country, judging by the growth of national income, agricultural and industrial production, employment and exports, remains strong and resilent. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drc40109b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
