\par\par \b \uc2  PRESS DIGEST - Indian newspapers - November 21. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,782 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  Following is a summary of major Indian business and political stories in leading newspapers prepared for Reuters by New Delhi-based Business News and Information Services Pvt Ltd. Telephone no. 11-332-4842, Fax 91-11-3351006 Internet: biznis.news@forums.sprintrpg.sprint.com X.400 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab     -----oOo-----\par \tab \tab     TOP STORIES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  The Hindustan Times \par \par \uc2  PRIME MINISTER SEEKS REPORT CARD ON DEVELOPMENTAL SCHEMES \par \par \uc2  In a move to step up the government's anti-poverty programmes, an important election plank of the Congress, the Prime Minister's Office has asked seven development-related ministries to submit report cards on central schemes by the 10th of every month. In a series of review meetings over the last 10 days, Prime Minister P.V. Narasimha Rao has indicated to government planners he was attaching top priority to various pro-poor schemes. \par \par \uc2  ----- \par \par \uc2  SHINDE, AHMED PATEL URGED TO BE STATE CONGRESS CHIEFS \par \par \uc2  The Pradesh Congress Executive Committees of the states of Maharashtra and Gujarat are expected to be announced shortly along with the new state Congress (PCC) presidents for the two states. All India Congress Committee general secretaries Sushil Kumar Shinde and Ahmed Patel are being persuaded to take over as PCC presidents in above states respectively. However, sources said both Shinde and Patel were not very keen to shift to state politics at this juncture when parliamentary elections are drawing near. \par \par \uc2  ----- \par \par \uc2  The Times Of India \par \par \uc2  CENTRE SAYS HELPLESS IN BREAKING MAFIA, POLITICIAN NEXUS \par \par \uc2  The government admitted there was a nexus between politicians, bureaucrats, individual criminals and the mafia but expressed its helplessness in improving the situation in view of the constitutional restrictions. Additional solicitor general Altaf Ahmand made this disclosure during the hearing of a public interest petition by Dinesh Trivedi, MP, seeking the Supreme Court's indulgence in getting the Vohra committee recommendations implemented. \par \par \uc2  ----- \par \par \uc2  The Pioneer \par \par \uc2  CIVIL CODE ONLY IF MINORITIES AGREE - HOME MINISTER \par \par \uc2  Union Home Minister S.B. Chavan declared that a uniform civil code would not be introduced if the minorities were not in favour of it. Addressing the Parliamentary Consultative Committee of the Home Ministry, Chavan assured the government would not allow the destruction of any religious places. The assurance followed a verbal clash among the members after BJP member V.K. Malhotra declared that Babri Masjid's demolition may be a matter of shame for "you", but for the BJP it was a matter of national pride. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab     -----oOo-----\par \tab \tab     MAIN BUSINESS STORIES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Economic Times\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BANKS ASKED TO FURNISH DAILY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  POSITION REPORT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) has instructed all commercial banks in the country to report their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  transactions on a daily basis. The directive was made effective from November 18. The banks have been told to spell out the buying and selling of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  both for spot and forward transactions. Besides, they are required to disclose the merchant deals related to exports and imports and the size of the inter-bank cover \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CORPORATE SECTOR GROWTH IN EXPORTS LOWER THAN IMPORTS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Exports by the corporate sector during 1994-95 grew at a lower rate than imports and domestic sales. This has resulted in a stagnancy in export intensity (exports as a percentage of net sales) at the cost of loss of net \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  due to higher growth of imports of raw materials. According to a study Export Performance of Companies in the Corporate Sector 1993-94 and 1994-95, prepared by the Industrial Credit and Investment Corporation of India, while the corporate sector's exports grew by 25.8 percent in the previous financial year, domestic sales jumped by 27 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ORISSA MINING CO LISTS 11 FOREIGN COS FOR GOLD, DIAMOND JVS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Orissa government owned Orissa Mining Corporation (OMC) has shortlisted 11 foreign companies to float joint ventures for the exploration and mining of gold and diamond in the state. The overseas companies are now making a presentation before an official committee. For gold mining, OMC has shortlisted seven foreign companies, sources said. They are Gold Mines, Gold Fields Ltd, Niguni Mining, all from Australia, Malaysia Mining Corporation, Kuala Lampur, High River Mines Ltd and Kilburn Engineering pacific Ltd of Canada and Inter Continental Business Development Ltd of United Kingdom.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Financial Express\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CHRYSLER 800 TO TAKE ON MARUTI CAR\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The US auto-giant Chrysler is keen to launch a 800 cc car in the Indian automobile market. According to industry sources, a top-level Chrysler team is to survey and subsequently prepare a project report on the automobile market. Significantly, the company's technology centre has already developed a prototype small car model which is expected to take on the Maruti 800.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par RBI PUMPS IN 36 BILLION RUPEES AS CALL RATE DIPS TO 20 PCT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the call-money market with repos of 36 billion via the apex body's agencies - the Discount and Finance House of India (DF&HI) and the Securities Trading Corporation of India (STCI). In the inter-bank call-market trades started at 30-37 percent, higher than last weekend's closing at 30 percent threabouts. Following the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , call-rates fell to 25 percent level and finally closed at lower than 20 percent. The fall in call-rates to 20 percent comes after a long period of heavy turbulence in call-rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par INCENTIVES BY POWER MINISTRY TO WOO INDEPENDENT PRODUCERS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Power Ministry has risen from its slumber to provide a realistic supportive framework to spur investments in projects, being pursued by Independent Power Producers (IPPs), by a series of measures announced in the last four weeks. The policy statements, coupled with proposed review of progress made in fast-track power projects by Prime Minister P.V.Narasimha Rao, demonstrates that the government is keen on encouraging at least a couple of promoters to achieve financial closure for three projects, which are under various stages of negotiation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TELECOM COM. TO REWORK RESERVE PRICES FOR BASIC SERVICES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Telecom Commission is reworking the reserve price for fresh bidding in the 10 telecom circles for basic services where the earlier bids were rejected as unreasonable. Telecom Commission chairman R.K.Takkar said the reworking is being done in the light of industry's suggestions in this matter. Among other things, he said, a note on the restructuring of Department of Telecommunications (DoT) will be placed before the Cabinet for approval. He refuted the charge that DoT was slowing down expansion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Business Standard\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par PROPOSAL TO RAISE PETROLEUM PRODUCT PRICES TURNED DOWN\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Prime Minister's Office is learnt to have shot down an inter-ministerial proposal to raise retail prices of petroleum products. The price increase is expected to be effected only after the elections. The proposal was mooted in view of the depreciation of the rupee by almost 10 percent since the beginning of September and its resultant impact on the oil pool account. It was pointed out that the oil import bill for the current financial year would be around $6.75 billion as against $5.94 billion during 1994-95. The rupee value of these imports will rise in a more pronounced manner to 225 billion rupees from 178.38 billion rupees, an in increase of 261 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HIMACHAL FUTURISTIC TOLD TO PICK CIRCLES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Department of Telecommunication has written to Himachal Futuristic Bezeq Communication asking it to convey the choice of the three A and B category circles it wants to retain out of the eight where it has emerged the highest bidder in the basic services tenders opened on August 31. The company has been given time till November 22 to convey its decision.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OPPENHEIMER FUND TO INVEST IN UNLISTED STOCKS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Oppenheimer, the frontline American foreign institutional investor, has mopped up $150 million in its recently-launched dedicated fund aimed at investing in unlisted Indian stocks. The fund, called India Equity Fund, will invest in stocks which are in the pre-initial public offer stage, and will hold them over a 12 to 24-month time frame.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par WORLD BANK WANTS IMPORT CURBS LIFTED\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A World Bank study says India has done a lot to open up its economy but has not removed restrictions on import of consumer goods. The study, authored by Nader Majid of the International Economics Department of the Bank's international trade division, said dismantling remaining barriers would help India and other countries in the region gain from the market opportunities in the post-Uruguay Round. Removing the remaining barriers would open up an estimated $7 billion (in 1992 dollar terms) a year in income gains, which would increase regional GDP by 2 percent and real wages by 2.5 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Observer\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par RESERVE BANK'S PRIORITY TO CHECK PRICE RISE: RANGARAJAN\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Reserve Bank Governor C.Rangarajan reiterated that the overriding concern of the monetary policy for 1995-96 would be to contain inflationary tendencies, but assured that credit would be available for productive purposes. The monetary policy would take into account the requirement of credit to support accelerated growth and also the prime objective of price stability. The nationalised banks will need to introduce innovative products while gearing up to face competition from private banks, non-banking financial companies and foreign banks in a deregulated environment, Dr Rangarajan said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Business Line\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FICS, MUTUAL FUNDS COOL TO SCICI RIGHTS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Foreign institutional investors (FIIs) and Indian mutual funds which collectively hold over 10 percent of the equity of SCICI Ltd, have declined to subscribe to the rights offer of the financial institution aggregating 13.11 billion rupees. FIIs, which hold around seven percent of the equity in SCICI, have decided to ignore the offering, given the depressed state of the capital market and the prospects of the company. Apart from Unit Trust of India, the response from other mutual funds to the issue has been lukewarm. The decision of FIIs not to subscribe to the offering has been attributed to the fact that it is a PCN (partly convertible note) issue.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par PETROLEUM SECTOR DRAFT POLICY RUNS INTO TROUBLE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Petroleum Ministry may have to tone down its proposed exploration and production policy considerably in the face of the strong reservations being expressed by the national oil companies and experts. The draft policy, in a bid to step up exploration effort and attract private sector interest, proposes radical changes such as PSU participation in bidding rounds, an open-acreage system with offers being evaluated on a quarterly basis and contracts awarded in three months, reduction of royalty and abolition of cess. However, the public sector companies, Oil and Natural Gas Corporation (ONGC) and Oil India Ltd (OIL), are opposed to the draft policy on various counts, say government sources.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (c) Reuters Limited 1995\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drbl01iqn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
