\par\par \b \uc2  (AWSJ): India Banks Expected To Raise Prime Lending Rate \b0\par\par\uc2 301 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 1995\par 01:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (AP-DJ)--Commercial banks in India are expected to raise their prime lending rates by about one percentage point within the next week, bankers and analysts say, Friday's Asian Wall Street Journal reports. \par \par \uc2  They cite sharp increases in short-term interest rates, rising term-deposit rates and higher demand for credit from industry. \par \par \uc2  Since January, banks have already raised their prime rates - those charged on loans to their best customers - by one to two percentage points, to an average of 16% to 16.5%. Other corporate borrowers get funds at 18% to 19%. \par \par \uc2  The immediate trigger for a prime increase is a sharp climb in interbank overnight call rates, the rate banks charge each other for overnight loans. Overnight call rates hit a high of 85% last Friday, dropped back down to about 50% earlier this week and spiked upward again Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since mid-October to support the rupee, which has fallen about 10% against the U.S. dollar since mid-August, sucked rupees out of the banking system and pushed overnight rates sharply higher from less than 20% previously. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent an estimated $500 million to $700 million supporting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by buying rupees in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Foreign banks are expected to lead the way in raising prime rates. With few branches and limited domestic-deposit bases, foreign banks are more sensitive to changes in interbank rates. \par \par \uc2  'There has been a rise in deposit rates. We will see their impact on the lending rates. We see a clear case for a one percentage point rise in prime rates,' says Ravi Nursey, treasury head at Dresdner Bank AG in Bombay. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 09-11-95 \par \par \uc2  0010GMT \par \par \uc2  1133 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drba08wy0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
