\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee Weak Despite RBI Dlr Sales, Money Rates Up \b0\par\par\uc2 214 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 1995\par 13:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI (AP-DJ)--The Indian rupee weakened slightly Thursday, despite small late-morning \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, while money market rates continued to spiral upwards over Wednesday's rates, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 34.65 rupees for the best part of the day Thursday, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sale of dollars for rupees brought the rate down from 34.70-34.75 rupees, a trader at a U.S. bank said. \par \par \uc2  Late selling of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for rupees by commercial banks in mid-afternoon trading pushed the dollar down further to 34.55 rupees at 1130 GMT, but this was still higher than 34.25 rupees Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile overnight inter-bank call money rates continued to hover above 60%, with a significant amount of business done at 80%, another money market dealer at a U.S. bank said. \par \par \uc2  Another trader at a foreign bank said that overnight call money rates had touched 95%, but very few deals were done at that level. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, call money was trading at around 40%, after trading as high as 75% during the day. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 02-11-95 \par \par \uc2  1206GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drb208mfq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
