\par\par \b \uc2  PRESS DIGEST - Indian newspapers - October 30. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,422 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Prepared for Reuters by New Delhi-based Business News and Information Services Pvt Ltd. Telephone no. 11-332-4842, Telefax 91-11-3351006 Voicemail: +91-11-3761233 Internet: biznis.news@forums.sprintrpg.sprint.com X.400) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab      -----oOo-----\par \tab \tab \tab \tab TOP STORIES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  The Times of India \par \par \uc2  UNITED STATES MAY GIVE PAKISTAN MORE ARMS \par \par \uc2  External Affairs Minister Pranab Mukherjee has said the government is evolving a befitting response at the diplomatic and military levels to what he feels is a resumption of the United States' arms supplies to Pakistan. He said that even though the U.S. had said that this was a one-time waiver, the possibility of the latter giving more arms to Pakistan had not been ruled out. \par \par \uc2  Indian Express \par \par \uc2  RUPEE WILL NOT FALL FURTHER SAYS COMMERCE MINISTER \par \par \uc2  Allaying fears of a continuing downside of the rupee, Commerce Minister, P.Chidambaram asserted that the recent fall was no more than a correction and that the rupee could only appreciate hereafter. He said the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would strongly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem any speculative run on the value of the rupee and advised exporters not to hold back their earnings and importers against any panic accumulation of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Economic Times \par \par \uc2  SEBI MAY INTRODUCE LOCK-IN PERIOD FOR BOUGHT-OUT DEALS \par \par \uc2  The Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI) may introduce a lock-in period for issues placed through the bought-out route. This means the investment bankers who acquire shares from new promoters for subsequently issuing them to general investors though an offer for sale, will have to hold these shares for a certain interval. SEBI believes the investment bankers must take the risk for some time, before they can pass on the shares to public investors. The reason for such a move is to restrict investment bankers from bringing to the capital market issues which lack credibility. \par \par \uc2  FOREIGN INVESTMENT PROMOTION BOARD OKAYS 23 BLN RUPEE \par \par \uc2  REFINERY IN UTTAR PRADESH \par \par \uc2  The Foreign Investment Promotion Board (FIPB) has cleared a proposal for a 23 billion rupee refinery in Uttar Pradesh to be set up jointly by Shell International and Bharat Petroleum. The joint venture will have a foreign equity portion of around 5.98 billion rupees. The six-million-tonne green field refinery is expected to come up at Sultanpur. Shell already has an agreement with Bharat Petroleum to sell its lubricants in India. \par \par \uc2  APRIL-SEPTEMBER TRADE DEFICIT UP AT $2,380 MLN \par \par \uc2  The trade deficit during April-September 1995 stood at $2,300 million compared with $1,230 million during the previous corresponding period. However, the monthly increase in the deficit rate has shown a decline since July 1995 and further moderation is expected in the second-half of the current year. Releasing India's foreign trade figures for the first six months of the current fiscal, Minister of State for Commerce P.Chidambaram, said the month-over-month increase in the trade deficit, which peaked at $702 million in July, had dipped to $409 million in September. \par \par \uc2  ITEMS RESERVED FOR SMALL-SCALE SECTOR TO BE REVIEWED \par \par \uc2  The Government has set up a committee of experts to review the list of 836 items reserved for the small-scale sector. Official sources said this would be a detailed exercise which would approach the question of de-reservation from the exports and technology upgradation angle. The committee has been given three months time, and sources said the status papers on various industries, currently under reservation, were under preparation. \par \par \uc2  MORGAN STANLEY SOLICITS OFFERS FOR $1-B OMAN-INDIA PIPELINE \par \par \uc2  EQUITY \par \par \uc2  Morgan Stanley, investment banker for the Oman Oil Company, is preparing an offer document to raise $1 billion as equity for the Oman-India pipeline project. The offer, for participation in the ownership consortium of the $4-billion project, will be made to global energy majors as well as strategic and institutional investors. Gas Authority of India Limited (G.A.I.L.), the Indian gas distribution partner, will also be offered a part of the equity. However, it is uncertain whether G.A.I.L. will pick up the stake. \par \par \uc2  RENEGOTIATIONS WITH ENRON SLATED FOR NOVEMBER 1. \par \par \uc2  The Maharashtra government will hold renegotiations with the U.S. multinational, Enron, on the Dabhol power project on November 1 as per the conditions laid down by right wing Shiv Sena chief Bal Thackeray. The committee for renegotiation would be appointed within a few days and would comprise only experts and the concerned secretaries. No minister would be included in the renegotiation committee. The conditions for renegotiations, as proposed by Thackeray, include limitation on power tariff, suitable compensation for farmers whose land had been acquired, transparency in negotiations and no environmental damage to Konkan area. \par \par \uc2  ESCORTS PLANS CONSORTIA TO BID FOR INFRASTRUCTURE PROJECTS \par \par \uc2  The 22 billion rupees Escorts group is planning to form a consortia, with leading international players, to bid for major infrastructure development projects. Arvind N Agrawal, executive director, Escorts Construction Equipment Ltd and head of Escorts Construction Equipment Business Group, said Escorts will bid for a number of projects including port modernisation and airport development projects, commercial and industrial parks as well as bypasses and bridges. \par \par \uc2  Financial Express \par \par \uc2  WORLD BANK REPORT FINDS PUBLIC SECTOR MEMORANDUM OF \par \par \uc2  UNDERSTANDING SYSTEM INEFFECTIVE \par \par \uc2  The World Bank has dumped the Memorandum of Understanding system in public sector undertakings as ineffective in improving the return on assets, labour productivity and total factor productivity of the companies. The bank has faulted the Indian Government and public managers for setting flawed targets resulting in lowering of total factor productivity (T.F.P.) to below pre-contract levels. According to a World Bank paper, T.P.F. measures the efficiency with which all factors are used and is the most reliable indicator of whether the contracts were associated with performance improvements. \par \par \uc2  RESERVE BANK PLANS BIG PUSH TO PROP UP RUPEE \par \par \uc2  Haunted by the looming spectre of a Mexico-type crisis, the Reserve Bank of India has put together a megaplan to crush increasing speculation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and prop up the rupee. The big push which is expected to be put into action on Monday has been conceived in concert with the top mandarins of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been designed to aim at propping up the rupee from the current level of 35.60 and keep it between 33.50 and 34.00 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Business Standard \par \par \uc2  GUIDELINES FOR TAKEOVER OF OLD POWER PLANTS ANNOUNCED \par \par \uc2  In a bid to augment availability of power in a quicker and cheaper way, the government has formulated detailed guidelines for private participation for renovation and modernisation of thermal and hydel power projects. The guidelines envisage three options which include lease, rehabilitate, operate and transfer, sale of plant and joint venture between state electricity boards and private companies. \par \par \uc2  FINANCE FIRMS WITHOUT RATING FACE ACTION \par \par \uc2  Almost 65 percent of non-banking financial companies (NBFCs), of 20 million rupees and above and registered with the Reserve Bank of India, may face deregisteration for not complying with the directive obliging them to obtain a credit rating. Notwithstanding the extension of the deadline, only 110 of the 312 registered large NBFCs have so far undergone a credit rating for their deposit scheme through an approved credit rating agency, as stipulated by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  MINING INDUSTRY MOST LUCRATIVE FOR OVERSEAS INVESTMENT \par \par \uc2  Price Waterhouse, the international financial audit and management consultancy firm, has identified the Indian mining industry as the most lucrative area of investment for overseas mining and exploration companies. The World Mining Group (W.M.G.), the specialised Price Waterhouse arm that provides a wide range of services to mining industries on a global scale, will soon prepare a detailed report on the country's prospects for circulation among its clients. W.M.G. chairman Robert B Parsons said the audit firm is particularly keen to guide foreign mining interests to India's geological resources. \par \par \uc2  The Observer of Business and Politics \par \par \uc2  AT&T, US-WEST LOBBYING FOR GETTING CELLULAR SERVICES TENDER \par \par \uc2  Multinational telecom giants AT&T and US-West are lobbying with the Prime Minister's Office to be awarded licences for providing cellular mobile services. Both the companies - along with their Indian partners, Birla and BPL - are trying to get minimum of three licences in category 'A' and 'B'. A Department of Telecom official said both the companies favoured no cap on cellular service licences but, due to political implications, they considered a cap of three. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc