\par\par \b \uc2  Indian government says rupee has fallen enough. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nelson Graves \par \uc2 574 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI, Oct 29 (Reuter) - India sought to shore up a weakening rupee on Sunday with a blunt warning to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators and a plea to traders to ignore predictions by some analysts that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will fall further. \par \par \uc2  Commerce Minister P. Chidambaram said the rupee, which has lost 12 percent in value against the U.S. dollar in two months, had declined too much and was poised to rebound. \par \par \uc2  "I think given the soundness of the fundamentals of the Indian economy, there simply is no justification for any expectation that the rupee will decline or ought to decline," Chidambaram told reporters. \par \par \uc2  He said the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI), the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if speculators tried to push the rupee down more. \par \par \uc2  "I have every reason to believe the RBI will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  strongly if speculative activities tend to push down the value of the rupee," Chidambaram said. \par \par \uc2  He said the trade picture was brightening, with exports growing strongly and a surge in imports tapering off. \par \par \uc2  On Friday afternoon the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded in a narrow range of about 35.55 to the dollar, well below its level of 31.4 which had held for some two years until August. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday helped take the rupee off its low of 35.72, but dealers said six-month forward dollars were quoted at more than 38 rupees. \par \par \uc2  Bunched debt repayments and growing oil import bills have weakened the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The fall gained momentum when dollar supplies thinned as the inflow of foreign funds slowed and exporters held back repatriation hoping for better rates. \par \par \uc2  The rupee's fall has sent shivers through Indian stock markets. Foreign institutional investors, the main buyers in the bourses in the past few months, were caught unaware by the abrupt fall in the rupee which has eroded their portfolios. \par \par \uc2  Chidambaram, one of the architects of Prime Minister P.V. Narasimha Rao's four-year-old economic reform programme, said the government shared his view the rupee had fallen enough. \par \par \uc2  "It is my view that anything beyond 34.50 (rupees per dollar) is perhaps an overcorrection," he said. "That is shared by the government, that an overcorrection may have already taken place and if anything, the rupee will appreciate." \par \par \uc2  Chidambaram, noting the six-month premium on the dollar was at an all-time high on Friday of almost 14 percent, appealed to importers not rush their purchases in anticipation they will soon cost more. "There is enough \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  He told exporters not to hold back on purchases. \par \par \uc2  "Exporters should bring back export earnings as soon as possible," he said. "If both importers and exporters continue to harbour these completely unjustified expectations, then the rupee will decline and both will be hurt." \par \par \uc2  Chidambaram said inflows of foreign direct investment and portfolio investment remained strong, and a surge in imports earlier in the year, especially of petroleum, newsprint and fertiliser, had tapered off. \par \par \uc2  Imports soared 50 percent in June compared with the same month in 1994, but the growth was 14 percent in September. \par \par \uc2  He predicted India's current account deficit would be between one and 1.5 percent of gross domestic product (GDP) in the 1995/96 financial year ending next March 31, higher than 0.8 percent last year but well within manageable limits. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drat02fu4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
