\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee/Stabilizes -2: Traders See Shift In RBI Tactics \b0\par\par\uc2 237 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 1995\par 14:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  As trading continued into the afternoon, the Reserve Bank of India again sold dollars for rupees, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed. \par \par \uc2  The sales began after the dollar inched back to 35.60 rupees, and continued until the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell back to 35.50 rupees, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Some traders saw the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday as a shift in tactics. They said the RBI appeared Friday to be trying to defend the rupee at a specific level, rather than simply providing liquidity to keep the market orderly. \par \par \uc2  'It looks as if the whole idea is to get the dollar below a rate of 35.70-35.80 rupees,' a trader with a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  'The market has started respecting the Reserve bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' another dealer said, who added that the dollar's rates could start to decline next week if players become certain that the RBI will continue to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strongly. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that the RBI's seriousness could be seen in the size of the lots it sold, which increased Friday from previous days. \par \par \uc2  'The size of the lots is larger now... they're selling in $3 million-$5 million lots today as compared to smaller lots' of $1 million-$3 million last week, the European bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 27-10-95 \par \par \uc2  1254GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drar08f96\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\s