\par\par \b \uc2  India/Stock/Amplifier -2: RBI Hasn't Restored Trust \b0\par\par\uc2 258 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 1995\par 13:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Analysts note that the rupee's sudden drop - which follows nearly two years of almost unbroken stability against the dollar - has shocked many observers who had predicted only a slow and small decline in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year. \par \par \uc2  And some half-hearted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has done little to restore confidence, they add, noting that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has only sold very small amounts of dollars that haven't managed to stop the rupee's slide. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the RBI confirmed that it continued to sell dollars for rupees in local markets Thursday. Though the bank didn't comment on the size of the sales, traders who dealt with the banks said it sold just $2-3 million a trade, which wasn't enough to help lift the rupee. \par \par \uc2  'The problem is that the RBI is not willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a large way,' says Ashwini Agarwal, research director at S.S. Kantilal Ishwarlal Securities. 'They don't want to dip into their reserves for short-term benefits.' \par \par \uc2  Most observers attribute the rupee's weakness to a surge in dollar demand from importers of capital goods and raw materials used to fuel India's strong industrial growth this year. \par \par \uc2  The boost in imports widened India's trade deficit to $1.976 billion in the April-August period - the first five months of the financial year, from $798.5 million in the same months of last year. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 26-10-95 \par \par \uc2  1112GMT \par \par \uc2  1138 