\par\par \b \uc2  India's Rupee Slightly Weaker In Nervous Early Trading \b0\par\par\uc2 233 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 1995\par 07:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI (AP-DJ)--The Indian rupee opened slightly weaker against the dollar in nervous trading Thursday, as dealers continue to watch for fresh signs of weakness in the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI). \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted that the dollar traded at around 35.55 rupees at the start of dealings Thursday, but then moved up to about 35.64 rupees at 0530 GMT. Late Tuesday, the last trading day for most banks, the dollar traded at 35.55 rupees. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that activity has been light so far, but the said demand for dollars does seem to be fairly strong and should support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout the day. \par \par \uc2  Since Thursday is the first full working day after several holidays in various areas of India earlier this week, traders expect to see some pent up dollar demand during the day, which should support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above 35.50 rupees. \par \par \uc2  They added, though, that some players expect the RBI to come in to sell dollars on any serious break above 35.65 rupees, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't been seen yet Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly sold small amounts of dollars in recent trading sessions, apparently in an effort to reduce volatility. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 26-10-95 \par \par \uc2  0548GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025draq08dr