\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar demand pushes Indian rupee to record low. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shyam Kumar \par \uc2 411 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Oct 20 (Reuter) - A surge in demand for dollars pushed the Indian rupee to a record low of 35.70 to the dollar on Friday, defying a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  attempt to arrest the fall, market analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  (sold dollars) at the 35.30 and the 35.50 level but it didn't have any impact," said a dealer at a Bombay bank. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a slowdown in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows into the country precipitated the rupee's fall. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed afternoon trading at 35.60. It has lost almost 12 percent in value in two months. \par \par \uc2  While most bank dealers felt that the rupee would continue to slide if current dollar demand persisted, investment analysts said the current fall was not a long-term trend. \par \par \uc2  "There is no depth in these movements," said Ramnath Iyer, analyst at Peregrine Capital in Bombay. "This is the result of a short-term mismatch," he said. \par \par \uc2  Bank dealers said there was an all-round rise in demand for dollars from Indian corporates for imports and debt repayments before December, the end of the financial year for many international firms. \par \par \uc2  "The devaluation in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  value was expected as Indian exporters wanted to be compensated for the inflation in the domestic sector," said an analyst with an Indian brokerage. \par \par \uc2  "The fall has been mainly because of an imbalance in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows during the first half of the year," said an analyst with WI Carr, the broking arm of Banque Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  Imports in the first half of 1995/96 (April to March) rose 39 percent while exports grew by 29 percent from a lower base. "The resulting deficit led to demand for the dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  Debt repayments of the government and a few corporates due for the second quarter of 1995/96 made the imbalance worse. \par \par \uc2  Last year, when \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows were high, the RBI had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the rupee from appreciating. But this year, the RBI has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  only sporadically to limit the fall, encouraging dealers to believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  supported a gradual devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts believe the rupee will strengthen. \par \par \uc2  "The rupee can only strengthen from the current level," said Iyer of Peregrine Capital. \par \par \uc2  WI Carr also said it expected dollar inflows to improve in the second the half of current fiscal, strengthening the rupee in the process. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drak02nhe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
