\par\par \b \uc2  India rupee falls to all-time low of 35.35 per dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2 232 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Oct 20 (Reuter) - The Indian rupee fell to an all-time low of 35.35 to a dollar on continued demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from importers, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee, which has been under pressure for the past month, opened at around 35.15 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  India rupee falls to all-time low of 35.35 per =2 \par \par \uc2  "The corporates are on a buying spree but the demand is limited," said a Bombay bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  He said exporters were sitting on their dollars waiting for the rupee to fall further. \par \par \uc2  "There is no sign of RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet," said another dealer. The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday when the rupee threatened to touch 35.0 rupees to a dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 35.48/60 to a dollar in latest deals. "At this rate, the rupee would soon cross the 36 level," said a dealer. \par \par \uc2  The rupee fell to a record low of 35.10 last month, and has since been hovering between the 33.50 to 35 level. \par \par \uc2  Bankers feel the RBI allowed the rupee to fall to the 35 level to keep Indian exports competitive. They feel the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are "comfortable" with this rate. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay newsroom +91-22-285 2851 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drak02ne9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
