\par\par \b \uc2  Indian Rupee Opens Slightly Stronger; No Sign Of RBI \b0\par\par\uc2 244 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 1995\par 07:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI (AP-DJ)--The Indian rupee opened slightly stronger in slow early trading Wednesday, with fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) keeping trading extremely thin and cautious, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  So far, however, the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't been seen in the market, although the dollar remains just below its all-time high against the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and well within the range where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars on Monday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0500 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 34.80 rupees, just below the 34.90 rupee level struck in late trading Tuesday. On Monday, the RBI began selling dollars at 34.90 rupees, and continued to about 34.40 rupees. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the cautious nature of early trading indicates that the dollar could move in a fairly narrow range of 34.70 rupees to 34.85 rupees for much of the day. \par \par \uc2  But they added that dollar buying interest from importers remains on the sidelines, and will have to emerge at some point to give the dollar a lift toward its all-time high of 35.10-20 rupees and challenge the Reserve Bank to stop the move. \par \par \uc2  'There's still a lot of demand in the market; we'll have to wait and see when it comes,' a dealer at a private bank in Bombay said. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 18-10-95 \par \par \uc2  0513GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drai0856x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\pi