\par\par \b \uc2  PRESS DIGEST - Indian newspapers - October 17. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,437 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab TOP STORIES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Times of India\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  SUPREME COURT GRANTS BAIL TO SANJAY DUTT \par \par \uc2  Film star Sanjay Dutt, who has been imprisoned for more than 15 months at Bombay's high-security Arthur Road jail, has been granted bail by the Supreme Court. Setting aside judge J.N.Patel's order rejecting the star's plea for bail, a three-judge bench laid down four conditions for Sanjay Dutt's release -- besides furnishing a bail bond and sureties, he was directed to surrender his passport immediately and not to hamper the investigation, tamper with evidence, or threaten or influence witnesses in the Bombay blasts case. \par \par \uc2  ----- \par \par \uc2  The Hindustan Times \par \par \uc2  ELECTION COMMISSION PROPOSES PARLIAMENTARY POLLS IN APRIL \par \par \uc2  The Election Commission (E.C.) is understood to have recommended holding of parliamentary elections in April next year. This would mean that the election would be brought forward by two months as the lower house of Parliament is scheduled to complete its five-year term in June, 1996. Sources said that a letter regarding this was sent to the government after the Election Commission had elicited the views of all the recognised political parties. \par \par \uc2  ----- \par \par \uc2  Indian Express \par \par \uc2  ALL-PARTY MEET TO DISCUSS KASHMIR POLLS \par \par \uc2  The government has convened an all-party meeting on election prospects in Jammu and Kashmir and cancelled the leave of its employees to ensure their availability for poll duty in the terrorism-affected state. This marked a step-up in the government's preparations for holding elections in the state before December 15 and coincided with a resumed effort by the ruling Congress party high command to patch up differences between the rival factions of its state unit. \par \par \uc2  ----- \par \par \uc2  The Pioneer \par \par \uc2  INDO-U.S. TALKS ON SCIENCE AND TECHOLOGY BREAK DOWN AGAIN \par \par \uc2  Discussions between India and the United States on the umbrella Indo-U.S. Agreement on Science and Techology have once again broken down, putting into jeopardy a wide range of accords on energy between the two countries. The recent round of discussions were held between senior officials of the Indian Department of Science and Technology and a U.S. delegation led by Special Advisor to the President and Senior Director for Global Affairs on the National Security Council. \par \par \uc2  ----- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab \tab    TOP BUSINESS STORIES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Financial Express\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SELECTED COMPANIES ASKED TO DISCLOSE END-USE OF ISSUE FUNDS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In yet another move aimed at better investor protection,the Securities and Exchange Board of India (S.E.B.I.) has directed certain companies to file detailed reports on deployment of funds raised by them through public issues. The move is primarily aimed at reducing the possibility of diversion of funds for purposes other than those mentioned in the prospectus and delays in project implementations.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par AES TRANSPOWER TO RENEGOTIATE AGREEMENT WITH ORISSA\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par AES Transpower Director Tony Colman is leading a team of power experts to renegotiate the power purchase agreement (P.P.A.) with the government of the eastern Indian state of Orissa. The U.S. utility company, which is putting up a 410 mw coal-fired power plant in the Ib valley at an estimated cost of 19.94 billion rupees, has submitted a fresh proposal following pressures from the new state government to reduce power cost.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Economic Times\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK INTERVENES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO KEEP RUPEE AFLOAT AGAINST DOLLAR\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (R.B.I.), \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market as the rupee touched a low of 34.90 against the dollar. The market was taken by surprise as the rupee started gaining against the dollar and touched 34.05 following R.B.I. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The R.B.I. is said to have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at various levels - first at Rs 34.90 and then even at lower levels.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEW SYSTEM OF EXPORT IMPORT CLASSIFICATION RELEASED\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Commerce Ministry has released a new harmonious system of commodity classification based on the format adopted by the Customs Cooperation Council, Brussels. This system is prevalent all over the world and meets the standard prescribed under GATT. Commerce Minister P.Chidambaram said this would accord a greater degree of transparency to trade policy administration.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SPECTRUM POWER REJECTS COUNTER-GUARANTEE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Spectrum Power is the first fast track power developer which has offered to go ahead with its plant without a counter-guarantee. The U.S.-based power company, which is promoting a 208 mw combined cycle power plant in Andhra Pradesh, has opted out of the counter guarantee route to avoid inordinate delays leading to cost escalations and time overruns.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SENSEX TO TOUCH 6,200 BY NEXT MARCH: JARDINE FLEMING\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Jardine Fleming India Broking (J.F.I.B.) appears to be very bullish about the Bombay stock exchange sensitive index, the sensex. It has predicted that the sensex will touch 6,200 on March 31, 1996. The firm does not make any assessment of what the market capitalisation or the average monthly turnover in the secondary market will be by the same date.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par IDBI SEEKS APPROVAL TO EXTEND EXTERNAL COMMERCIAL BORROWING\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Industrial Development Bank of India (IDBI) has sought an extension of the external commercial borrowing approval it has secured from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for in June.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Business Standard\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FINANCE MINISTRY FORCES BANKS TO BUY GILTS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Finance Ministry has joined the Reserve Bank of India in arm-twisting public sector banks into buying government paper. The ministry has directed most public sector banks to bail out the 30 billion rupee 13.5 percent, two year maturity paper issued on October 14.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORWARD MARKETS REGULATOR BEING RECAST\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Forward Markets Commission (F.M.C.), the regulatory body for commodity markets and exchanges, is overhauling its own structure and revamping the functioning of the exchanges to give them a greater role as self-regulatory organisations (S.R.O.s). The move is in line with the recommendations of the Kabra Committee which said the F.M.C. should be recast on the lines of the Securities and Exchange Board of India.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEW NORMS PUT ONUS OF SAFETY ON AIRLINES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The directorate-general of civil aviation (D.G.C.A.) has issued fresh guidelines to domestic airlines, putting the onus for flight safety primarily on the airlines themselves and restricting the regulatory authority's role to taking corrective action. The guidelines issued under the civil aviation requirements apply to domestic airlines, air taxis and cargo operators.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SECURITIES PANEL ASKS MUTUAL FUND TO COMPLY WITH OFFER TERMS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI) had directed Canbank Mutual Fund to honour the terms of the offer document for its close-ended scheme Canstar. The fund had suddenly altered the terms of repurchase unilaterally since the scheme had come into existence before the regulations on mutual funds.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TEMPLETON TO STEP UP INVESTMENTS IN INDIA\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Franklin Templeton, the world's fourth largest fund manager with global assets worth nearly $130 billion, is set to increase its exposure in Indian paper manifold through the foreign institutional investors it operates in the country. Templeton Growth Fund, Templeton Emerging Markets Fund and Templeton Developing markets Fund, which have already invested close to $45 million in Indian securities, have decided to target key scrips in an effort to pick up large chunks of blue chip stocks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Observer of Business and Politics\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par COMPETITIVE BIDDING ESSENTIAL FOR POWER PROJECTS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Competitive bidding has been made mandatory for all private power projects for which memoranda of understanding (MoU) were signed after February 18, 1995 or letter of intents issued after the date. Power Secretary P.Abraham has reiterated the government's earlier instructions on competitive bidding and warned that non-compliance of the order would mean rejection of a proposal by the Central Electricity Authority.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Business Line\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BOMBAY BOURSE AMENDS CAPITAL ADEQUACY NORMS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bombay Stock Exchange (BSE) on Monday amended the capital adequacy norms for brokers. The bourse authorities have advised members to achieve the full 3 percent capital adequacy by November 1 this year. Earlier, brokers were allowed to achieve 2 percent capital adequacy by November this year and 3 percent by November 1996. B.S.E. also made other changes in the capital adequacy norms.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GOVERNMENT KEEN TO UPGRADE TWO MAJOR PORTS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The government is toying with the idea of developing two major ports, one each on the east and west coasts, into world-class ports to meet the requirements of trade and industry, according to M.Rajsekara Murthy, Minister of State for Surface Transport. Murthy said while foreign ports handled nearly 300 million tonnes of cargo annually, the total cargo throughput at all the major ports in the country was yet to cross the 200-million tonne mark.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (c) Reuters Limited 1995\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drah02rcb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
