\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  Rupee Steady In Quiet Trade; No  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  From RBI \b0\par\par\uc2 210 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 1995\par 11:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI (AP-DJ)--The Indian rupee held steady through quiet mid-afternoon trading Tuesday, as players reported a lack of dealing interest following the Reserve Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a slide in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday. \par \par \uc2  'It's been a very boring day actually,' a trader at a foreign bank in Bombay said. He noted that dealings have been confined to a narrow range, as players remain reluctant to stray very far from current levels for fear of inviting further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that the dollar opened at around 34.55 rupees, and then climbed slowly to 34.65 rupees before inviting dollar sales from exporters. Traders noted that exporters seemed keen to sell dollars around that level, driving the dollar back down to 34.55 rupees in mid-afternoon dealings, unchanged from late Monday's level. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the RBI began selling dollars around 34.90 rupees, and continued to sell small amounts down to about 34.40 rupees. Traders interpreted the unusual step as a measure to prevent the dollar from testing its all-time high of 35.10-20 rupees struck in September. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 17-10-95 \par \par \uc2  0932GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drah081n0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
