\par\par \b \uc2  India rupee seen weaker after dropping to new low. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sumit Sharma \par \uc2 545 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Sept 15 (Reuter) - The Indian rupee plunged to a record low against the dollar on Friday but recovered marginally even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refused to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , leaving the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to seek its natural level, bankers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee hit the 35 rupee per dollar level on Friday morning before recovering to 34 rupees on rumours of a planned \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI). \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered to fluctuate between 33.25 and 34.25 through the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Though there has been no official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , RBI officials said they were keeping "a very close watch" on the movements. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said in the near-term the rupee would not cross the 35-rupee level as most importers stayed away after the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit the all-time low in the morning. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like the rupee will trade in the range of 32-35 per dollar in the near future," said Rajendra Gill, treasury head with Bank of Nova Scotia in Bombay. \par \par \uc2  "But there will be a net demand for the dollars in the next few months," Gill said. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said the RBI was probably testing the waters before India introduces further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relaxations. The rupee was made convertible on current account in August last year, but the RBI has retained several restrictions on capital movement. \par \par \uc2  "The RBI is letting the rupee find its level since it goes well with its policy of liberalisation and as the rupee finally depreciates it gives exporters some incentive," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said that the sudden depreciation could create nervousness in the market. Though RBI stayed out of the market, they felt it could use "moral suasion" to cool sentiment. \par \par \uc2  "How can one remain unconcerned," a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offficial told Reuters when asked if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was planning to prevent a further fall of the rupee. \par \par \uc2  Indian finance secretary Montek Singh Ahluwalia said the fall is "just a normal market situation". He told Reuters, "I don't see any problem really." \par \par \uc2  The rupee's slide began after the Indian government said it would raise its entire requirement of foreign debt payments through the inter-bank \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said as most exporters had already sold their receivables forward, the immediate dollar supply was limited. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said foreign portfolio investments will not be adversely affected. \par \par \uc2  "It won't make much of a difference," said analyst Ramnath Iyer, with Peregrine Capital in Bombay. "Foreign funds expect 5-6 percent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation when they invest abroad." \par \par \uc2  In real terms the rupee had appreciated in the past two years because it was held steady, he said. \par \par \uc2  "The fall now is more like a correction," Iyer said. "It is not a devaluation in the Mexican sense. We expect the rupee to stabilise between 33.2 and 33.5." \par \par \uc2  Most industry analysts welcomed depreciation of the rupee. \par \par \uc2  "The impact should be beneficial for indian industry in general because it will provide extra protection against imports," said R. Balakrishnan, head of research with DSP Financial Consultants, an Indian associate of Merrill Lynch. \par \par \uc2  "Foreign portfolio investment could rise as Indian stocks look cheaper in dollar terms," Balakrishnan said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr9f03xy5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
