\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  panel favours selective RBI  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 176 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, July 7 (Reuter) - A committee set up by the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market reforms said it favoured selective \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central banking authority should be selective rather than continuous," it said. \par \par \uc2  "It is therefore recommended that there should be no statutory obligation as it exists today on the RBI to fix its buying and selling rates on a daily basis and instead it should be enabled to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only when required in keeping with the objectives of its policy," the committee said. \par \par \uc2  The RBI offers a daily buying and selling rate of 31.37 and 31.53 rupees per dollar to the banks. RBI had been continously present in the market on the buying side until February 1995. \par \par \uc2  "Although this policy has helped to keep the dollar-rupee levels stable, it is market forces which should determine the value of home \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the committee said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr77065cp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
